  "model": "gemini-4-carbon",
  "contents": "Is this clause enforceable in a residential lease in Ontario? Cite the section.\n\nRead the clause as a term of an ordinary private residential tenancy in Ontario that the Residential Tenancies Act, 2006 covers: the rental unit has no exemption under section 6.1, and it is not student housing, a care home, a co-operative or a mobile home park.\n\nClause: Rent shall be paid by pre-authorized debit from the Tenant's bank account on the first day of each month.\n\nEnd your answer with these two lines:\nVerdict: enforceable (or: Verdict: not enforceable)\nSection: <the number of the one section of the Residential Tenancies Act, 2006 that decides it>\n",
  "config": {
   "max_output_tokens": 16000
  }
 },
 "requested_at": "2026-11-13T20:10:31.880730Z",
 "text": "No, this clause is not enforceable. \n\nUnder section 108(b) of the *Residential Tenancies Act, 2006*, neither a landlord nor a tenancy agreement can require a tenant or prospective tenant to permit the automatic debiting of their account at a financial institution (such as pre-authorized debit) or any other form of automatic payment for the payment of rent. While a landlord and tenant can voluntarily agree to use pre-authorized debit as a payment method, a lease clause that makes it mandatory is contrary to the Act and therefore void.\n\nVerdict: not enforceable\nSection: 108",
